<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henri Becquerel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1852">1852</a>–<a href="/years/1908">1908</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Antoine Henri Becquerel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antoine Henri Becquerel was a French experimental physicist, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> into a family of physicists, who discovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radioactivity">radioactivity</a>. While investigating phosphorescence after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-rontgen">Wilhelm Röntgen</a>'s discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-rays</a>, he found in early 1896 that uranium salts left in a dark cabinet still exposed photographic plates, and by May he concluded that the radiation came from the uranium itself. He shared the 1903 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics with Marie and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a>, and also identified beta particles as high-speed electrons. He died at Le Croisic in 1908, aged 55, with serious burns on his skin reported, likely from radioactive materials. The SI unit of radioactivity is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henri-becquerel.jpg" alt="Henri Becquerel" width="330" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Nadar / Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul_Nadar_-_Henri_Becquerel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antoine Henri Becquerel was born on 15 December 1852 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> into a family of physicists: his grandfather Antoine César Becquerel, his father Edmond Becquerel and his son Jean Becquerel were all notable. He entered the École polytechnique in 1872 and the École des ponts et chaussées in 1874, became an engineer in 1877 and in 1892 was appointed Professor of Applied Physics at the National Museum of Natural History. In the early 1880s he began research on luminescence, and in 1888 he received a doctorate from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a> for this work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Becquerel learned of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-rontgen">Wilhelm Röntgen</a>'s discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-rays</a> on 20 January 1896, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-poincare">Henri Poincaré</a> read Röntgen's preprint to the French Academy of Sciences. He thought that phosphorescent materials, including uranium salts, might emit penetrating radiation when lit by bright sunlight, and through February he exposed wrapped photographic plates under coins and phosphorescent material to the sun. The days of 26 and 27 February were overcast, so he left the plates in a dark drawer, and on 1 March he developed them and found the silhouettes as distinct as before. He reported this on 2 March without claiming more.</p><p>By May 1896, after experiments with non-phosphorescent uranium salts, he reached the correct explanation, that the radiation came from the uranium itself without any external source of energy. He published seven papers on the subject in 1896, and the research that followed led to the discovery of polonium and radium by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-curie">Marie Curie</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a>. In 1900 he found that beta particles matched high-speed electrons, and the following year he left a piece of radium in his vest pocket and was burnt by it, an observation that led to radiotherapy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Becquerel died on 25 August 1908 at Le Croisic, aged 55, of a heart attack, and it was reported that he had developed serious burns, likely from handling radioactive materials. A crater on the Moon, a crater on Mars and the mineral becquerelite are named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henri Becquerel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
